WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Once you start making more money, the

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:07.840
easiest mistake is upgrading the wrong

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.800
things.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.400
You end up spending because you can

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.720
afford it. But there is a better way to

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.280
spend.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.600
I spent years on Wall Street and as a

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:21.640
CEO making [music] tough calls about

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.320
money. So I learned these lessons by

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:28.440
getting it wrong a lot.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.640
Your next dollar has four possible jobs.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.800
Four categories [music]

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.640
worth upgrading immediately. And the

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.040
last one has the worst financial [music]

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.080
return.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.575
And that one matters the most.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.800
Money actually can buy you happiness.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:46.640
>> [music]

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.640
>> Just not the way we think.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.040
In 2010, Nobel Prize winning

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.200
psychologist Daniel Kahneman suggested

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.640
that happiness has a ceiling.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.400
According to his research, when you

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.080
reach $75,000 a year,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.640
more money doesn't seem to improve your

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:08.960
day-to-day emotional well-being. And for

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.640
more than a decade, everyone quoted that

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.200
number. Except that it turns out we were

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:17.040
all reading it wrong. So in 2023,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.760
Kahneman teamed up with another

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.760
researcher and they both arrived at a

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.880
surprising conclusion.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.640
For most people, more money

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.640
does keep making life better. But the

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.400
relationship between money and happiness

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.520
is not a straight line.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:37.680
Let's say you get X amount of lift in

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:41.520
your happiness by going from 40,000 to

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:47.000
80,000. Now the same lift will happen

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:49.280
only when you go from 80,000 to 160,000.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.720
And then if you want a similar lift once

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.640
again, then you have to go from 160 to

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.200
320,000.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.640
So it just takes more and more money to

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.760
provide the same hit.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.040
It seems like money behaves just like

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.400
any drug.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.280
The economic principle here is called

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.840
diminishing marginal utility. The next

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.120
dollar has to work harder than the last

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:16.240
one because it's worth less to you.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.480
I love ice cream, so I know this well.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.120
Your fifth ice cream cone won't give you

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.720
the same pleasure as your first one.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.600
Believe me, I've tried.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:30.520
So, being rich and living a life that's

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.080
rich in meaning are two entirely

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.400
different things.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.680
If your net worth rises while your

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.800
self-worth falls, you're just rich on

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:41.720
paper.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.959
We want to talk about where will my next

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.360
dollar create the most additional value

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:49.600
for me? Can the additional money buy

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.160
back your health, your time, your

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.600
attention?

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.360
Can it increase your future earning

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.080
potential? Can it make your

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.840
relationships, [music] your experiences,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.040
and your actual life richer?

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.120
I call it the 4S framework. So, let's

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.680
use this framework [music] to figure out

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.080
what is actually worth upgrading

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.920
and why?

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.640
First, stamina.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:18.000
What if you showed up at work completely

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.280
drunk? What would that do to your

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.800
career?

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.760
But that's exactly what happens to most

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.800
of us who work hard.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:30.120
We just don't know it. In one of those

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.640
landmark sleep studies, two sleep

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.239
researchers [music] in Australia kept

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.519
people awake through the night and

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:40.760
repeatedly tested their reaction time

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:43.040
and their [music] coordination. Then,

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.840
they compared those results

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.239
with people who had been drinking.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.120
By the end of the night, the sleep

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:55.920
deprived [music] group was performing as

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:58.440
badly as the people who were drunk.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.400
And the longer they stayed awake, the

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.320
worse it got.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.960
We all know that our ability to succeed

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:08.880
depends on making sound decisions, which

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:12.440
depends on how this machine up here

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.760
works, our brain. Before you upgrade

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.320
what you own,

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.280
upgrade the machine that earns it. When

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.600
you under spend in this category, you'll

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.720
see the adverse effect in everything

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.680
downstream. And the most important

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:30.360
upgrade is your sleep habits. And

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.919
I'm not good at this. I stay up late

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.320
knowing I shouldn't, but also

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:36.800
I don't want to wake up early in the

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.000
morning anymore.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.960
There

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.280
was a time when I used to get up in the

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.520
morning and I would have this really bad

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.919
back pain.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:51.120
And I remember spending days researching

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:53.280
to find the right mattress and pillows

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:57.360
combination. They were more expensive,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:05:00.200
but why skimp on that? You spend a third

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.480
of your life in that bed. And that time

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:05.080
decides the quality of the other two

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.880
thirds of your life when you're up. The

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.560
underlying principle is to spend on

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.840
things that improve the quality of your

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.760
decisions.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.440
Now, the second principle is adjacent

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.480
to. Spend money when it makes the right

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.760
behavior easier.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:25.160
You can buy a good habit when you know

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:28.440
that you won't build it yourself. For

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:30.560
instance, let's say you already know

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.280
that you should work out.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.480
You won't find time for it somehow.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.480
Same here.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.760
But maybe you put a stake in the ground

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:43.200
by spending more on a great gym that you

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:45.440
will be more likely to go to.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.600
If that doesn't work, maybe you can hire

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.640
a trainer who comes to you.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.040
They will show up even if you don't want

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:54.000
to.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.040
So, that's how you buy a good habit. And

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.440
the third principle, spend on the

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.200
bottleneck.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.040
I realized I would work all day long

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:07.160
while sitting in chairs and couches.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:09.120
So, I bought myself a standing desk. And

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:11.640
I've always been able to work in messy

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.640
rooms. And it doesn't bother me. But, I

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:17.880
can't work when there's noise or music

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.960
playing. So, one upgrade that I made was

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.360
a really good pair of noise-canceling

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.400
headphones.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.800
They were

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.200
more expensive than Apple's EarPods, but

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.640
to me,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:33.640
finding that silence was priceless.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:36.400
Most people use money to upgrade their

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:39.640
lifestyle. I think it's better to

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.760
upgrade your capacity to live it and

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:45.280
enjoy it. So, that's our first S for

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.080
stamina. You know, my mentor used to say

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.760
this a lot. If you want to make more

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:52.120
money, don't upgrade your lifestyle,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.480
upgrade your life skills.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:59.120
Long time ago, I was trying to move into

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:01.360
Wall Street. I had spent my career in

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.720
technology and [music] I was at a

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.720
company that was acquired. And it was a

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:09.440
great exit and I thought I should do

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.160
something different. Now, I had an MBA,

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:14.800
but I had not paid attention to [music]

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.520
corporate finance courses when I was at

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.200
MIT.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.400
That was something that I wasn't feeling

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:24.040
very confident about. So, I started

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.120
taking courses for CFA certification.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:29.919
And I started teaching myself how to

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:32.480
build complex valuation models, how to

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:34.400
analyze companies, how to [music] read

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.280
financial statements, and so on and so

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.480
on.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.200
And I did that for a long time and then

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:42.360
I started applying. And I got interviews

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.920
from three very large [music] banks and

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:48.120
each one of them asked me about my

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:50.520
experience. And I would just say,

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.600
"Listen, I don't have a lot of

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.080
experience [music] in finance. So, the

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.240
best way is for you to give me a

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.920
difficult financial modeling

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:01.280
assignment."

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:04.560
And I would then deliver it in three or

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:06.720
four days. And I wasn't some insanely

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.480
brilliant guy or anything. It's just

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.200
that I had practiced it over and over

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:13.520
again before I showed up for the

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.240
interview. And then those years in

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:20.040
investment banking helped me understand

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:23.000
businesses at a macro level, and I was

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:25.919
able to do C-level roles afterwards,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:28.840
leading to a CEO position,

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:31.800
and then board seats.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:34.919
And the key principle here is this:

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:38.039
Invest in foundational [music] skills

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:40.880
that bring you recurring benefits.

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:42.040
Finance 101, communications,

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.159
negotiations,

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:45.440
>> [music]

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:47.880
>> time management. The best skills don't

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.400
pay you once. They keep paying you

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:53.839
forever. [music]

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:56.200
So, spend your money upgrading those.

00:08:53.839 --> 00:08:58.240
And you know, in the future that's

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:01.400
heading our way,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:04.640
the biggest differentiator for you will

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:06.000
be your constant ability to learn new

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.960
skills.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:12.080
Because AI will keep commoditizing a lot

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:13.680
of skills you have already built today.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.240
So, you have to keep evolving. You have

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.800
to keep adapting. The important question

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.640
that you can ask every month is this:

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.080
What skill gap is currently costing me

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.520
the most?

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:27.160
That's where your money should be

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.880
invested. Every 6 months,

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:32.720
create a learning budget,

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:35.520
both in terms of money and time. Take

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.280
courses online, or join an evening

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.800
school, or learn from mentors. You know,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.360
one of the best upgrades that I made in

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.720
my life was my mentors.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.320
When I became the CEO, I found a mentor

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:51.880
who had just retired after building a

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.560
company worth $60 billion.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:56.720
So, just one conversation with him

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.240
across the table was able to give me

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.840
perspectives that I wouldn't have been

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:02.640
able to discover for myself for years to

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.240
come.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.080
The best thing you can buy is something

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:10.640
no one can take away from you.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.040
And now, we go to the third job,

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.480
security.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:17.480
The richer you get, the more expensive

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:20.040
your mistakes become. Making money and

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:23.560
keeping money require totally different

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:26.187
skills. In 2021, everybody was talking

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:26.440
about this hedge fund called Archegos.

00:10:26.187 --> 00:10:29.280
>> [music]

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:32.320
>> And they were riding high. They had a

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.760
portfolio of stocks that looked so

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.080
spectacularly successful.

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:39.720
Tens of billions of dollars.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.907
But, there was one problem.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.839
Most of that portfolio was financed

00:10:41.907 --> 00:10:44.880
[music]

00:10:42.839 --> 00:10:46.492
with borrowed money. It's called

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.520
leverage in finance.

00:10:46.492 --> 00:10:49.120
>> [music]

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:51.600
>> So, when a few of those stocks in the

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:53.720
fund started going south,

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.880
the fund could not meet the margin

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.000
calls.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.920
And banks [music] rushed to sell the

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:02.867
collateral, the entire enterprise

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.920
collapsed within days. Credit Suisse

00:11:02.867 --> 00:11:07.000
>> [music]

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:08.720
>> lost 5 and 1/2 billion dollars.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.720
Never recovered.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.480
So, here's the principle that I always

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:17.160
remember. Wealth is often built by

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:20.640
making smart, huge bets, but it's

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:23.120
preserved by making smart hedges.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.040
When I was a teen,

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:29.440
I ran away from my house,

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:33.200
and I was homeless for a while.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:36.520
And I know what zero feels like.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:40.160
And maybe that's why once I finally

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:42.840
build some financial distance from zero,

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:45.800
I've always asked one question.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:48.400
What could send me back there? And you

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:52.440
have to hedge against three key risks.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:55.760
First, concentration. I had a personal

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:59.120
and painful experience with this risk. I

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:03.800
held a large position in a single stock.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:06.080
And the stock went from $19 to 140.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.080
And my advisor started telling me, "Hey,

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:11.560
you got to implement some hedging

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:14.160
strategies to protect the downside." But

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.760
I was so bullish, and I didn't do

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.080
anything about it.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:21.840
And when the fall came, man, it came so

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.760
fast and furious. We had no time to

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:27.160
adjust and recover.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:30.560
So, once you have real savings,

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:33.080
upgrade the savings by diversifying and

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:35.920
hedging your risks. Keep enough

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:38.520
emergency funds, so if a market crash

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:40.839
happens, or there's a job loss, or

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:42.960
you've had a bad year, it doesn't force

00:12:40.839 --> 00:12:45.760
you to take more risks.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:48.800
Those extra dollars should buy you peace

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:51.120
of mind. Second, constraints. Now, this

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:54.040
is another upgrade in terms of security

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:57.800
that I learned late in my life. I call

00:12:54.040 --> 00:13:01.320
it the BATNA fund. BATNA is a term that

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:04.360
I learned from negotiators. Your better

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:07.200
alternative to negotiated agreement.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:09.839
BATNA. Or simply put, your ability to

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.880
have a better option in your back pocket

00:13:09.839 --> 00:13:13.960
when you're negotiating the current one.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:16.160
So, I was able to walk away from a job,

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:20.360
for example, that didn't excite me

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:22.600
because I had saved enough money to get

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:23.800
through six or nine months of job

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:26.560
search.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:29.280
Upgrade your BATNA fund.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:32.040
And finally, complexity. Once your

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:35.240
finances become meaningfully more

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:38.560
complex, it's a good idea to upgrade to

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:40.720
a tax accountant, for example, or even

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.440
hire a financial planner.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:43.880
They can help you work through your

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:46.240
choices.

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:48.280
A good attorney can also make sense

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:50.720
because you can then take care of your

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:52.640
estate plan, and you should always have

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:56.240
an estate plan because that's your way

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:58.120
to provide security to those you love.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.560
A very good friend of mine, who's a CEO

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:02.440
of a very large company in LA, lost his

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:06.000
entire house, including all the

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:09.160
furniture, to the fires in Los Angeles.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:12.040
And he and his family had to rebuild

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:15.160
their entire life from scratch.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:17.800
But, he had planned his life well.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:21.360
He had allocated his extra money for

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:23.880
those specific risks, so he didn't have

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:26.680
to start back from zero. But, stuff like

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:29.680
that can happen to any of us, and that's

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.040
why upgrading your financial security is

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:33.720
the best move.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:36.960
Now, once you've done that, there is one

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:40.080
final question left. What exactly are

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.520
you protecting all of this for? This

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:45.480
last upgrade has the worst financial

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:48.280
returns, and that's the best part.

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:51.800
That's our fourth S, soul.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:54.760
Being rich and living a life that's rich

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.839
in meaning are two entirely different

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:58.040
things.

00:14:55.839 --> 00:14:59.960
And there are three places where an

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:03.160
upgrade to make your life genuinely

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:04.680
richer makes sense. The first is for

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:07.839
yourself.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:10.520
Spend money on things you love. Things

00:15:07.839 --> 00:15:12.720
that truly light you up.

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:15.160
Music, painting, cooking, golf,

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:19.160
photography, whatever it is. I was

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:19.160
trained in Indian classical music.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:24.880
When I got to a point when I could

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:26.960
afford it, I paid for the courses at

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:29.720
Berklee School of Music in Boston

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:32.680
because I wanted to learn about Western

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:36.120
principles of harmony and melody. And I

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:38.640
got to learn from a world-class faculty.

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:40.840
I learned how they think about music,

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:42.560
how they feel about music, how they see

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:45.600
music.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:47.640
And those courses [music] are expensive,

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:51.120
but those lessons

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:53.720
enriched my life in so many ways.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:56.880
It was an amazing experience. You can

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:58.240
find and fund your amazing experiences

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:01.120
the same way.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:03.840
It's part of being generous toward your

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:05.320
own self. The second upgrade is for the

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:09.200
people you love.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:11.960
There is an old quip I heard somewhere.

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:15.760
A seeker asked a wise man,

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:17.480
"What matters more in life, the journey

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:20.240
or the destination?"

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:21.360
And the wise man says,

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:24.320
"Neither.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:25.680
What matters is the person you travel

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.839
with."

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.960
In 1938,

00:16:27.839 --> 00:16:32.440
Harvard researchers started following

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:35.680
hundreds of people across their entire

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:38.800
adult life. They tracked their careers,

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:40.920
health, marriages, failures, successes,

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:43.320
everything. That study, by the way, is

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:46.280
still running today, almost 90 years

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:49.000
later. Years into the research, they

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:52.320
asked a simple question.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:55.440
"What factors in our midlife best

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:57.480
predicts our happiness and health when

00:16:55.440 --> 00:17:01.080
we're 80?"

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:04.480
One factor stood out again and again.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:06.680
The quality of our relationships.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.920
The people who were more satisfied in

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:12.600
their relationships at 50

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:15.040
were the healthiest at 80.

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:17.800
So, invest your [music] time and money

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:20.240
for those who care about you.

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:23.319
Have dinner with your life partner. Go

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:25.000
see a Broadway show together. Take long

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:27.360
walks.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.520
And same for the family. Take your kids

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:33.800
somewhere they'll remember.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.800
Create the moment that becomes a story.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:39.280
Because that's all you're going to

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:41.480
remember later.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:44.800
When you can afford to upgrade your

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:46.320
life, please upgrade your generosity for

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.920
others as well.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:52.320
Help a stranger. Support something you

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:54.600
believe in. Volunteer. Build someone so

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:57.120
they can believe in themselves.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:59.320
And it doesn't have to be just money,

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.520
right? It could be your time.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:04.880
So, that's the framework. Stamina,

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:06.960
skills, security, and soul. And now you

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:10.720
can go ahead and make your own list of

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:13.920
upgrades that fit into these categories.

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:15.320
You know, one of my favorite movies is

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:19.800
Arrival.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:22.840
And at its heart, it asked a really hard

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:25.760
and almost heartbreaking question.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:27.080
If you knew something would eventually

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:29.920
end,

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:31.360
would you still choose to go ahead with

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.800
it?

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:37.680
You may have your own answer,

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:41.360
but my answer is, yes, of course.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:45.040
The value of life or relationship

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:47.720
does not come from its permanence.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.577
Our time here is brief.

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:49.000
We know that.

00:18:48.577 --> 00:18:52.640
>> [music]

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:54.080
>> But that brevity doesn't reduce its

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:57.840
beauty.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:59.713
Maybe that's the ultimate upgrade.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.920
Using what you have

00:18:59.713 --> 00:19:04.360
>> [music]

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:04.360
>> to build a life that matters to you.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:08.720
Thank you.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.720
And I love [music] you.

